-- ID 617: Prospective Analysis — Smoothing constant responsiveness analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Meridian Electronics uses exponential smoothing to forecast monthly sales. In January, the forecast was $200,000 and actual sales were $240,000. The operations team runs two models: Model A uses a smoothing constant (alpha) of 0.3 and Model B uses alpha of 0.7. Analyze how the choice of alpha affects each model''s February forecast and explain when a higher alpha is preferred over a lower one.',
  choices = '["Model A forecasts $212,000 and Model B forecasts $228,000 — the higher alpha in Model B places more weight on the recent actual ($240,000), making the forecast more responsive to the demand spike; a higher alpha is preferred when recent data reflects a genuine trend shift, while a lower alpha is preferred when fluctuations are noise rather than signal", "Both models produce identical forecasts of $220,000 because alpha only affects forecasts three or more periods ahead", "Model A forecasts $228,000 and Model B forecasts $212,000 because a lower alpha reacts more aggressively to recent data", "Model A forecasts $240,000 and Model B forecasts $200,000 because each model ignores either the forecast or the actual value entirely"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Exponential smoothing calculates the new forecast as F(t+1) = alpha * Actual + (1 - alpha) * Forecast. Model A: 0.3 * $240,000 + 0.7 * $200,000 = $212,000. Model B: 0.7 * $240,000 + 0.3 * $200,000 = $228,000. The higher alpha weights recent actuals more heavily, producing a forecast closer to the latest observation. This responsiveness is beneficial when demand patterns are genuinely shifting (e.g., new product launch), but harmful when recent data contains random noise — a high alpha would chase every fluctuation. Conversely, a low alpha smooths out noise but reacts slowly to real trend changes. Choice C reverses the alpha-weight relationship. Choice D incorrectly assumes the models ignore one input entirely, when both always blend the prior forecast with the actual.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 617;

-- ID 619: Prospective Analysis — Regression extrapolation reliability analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Cascade Analytics builds a regression model for Westbrook Manufacturing''s production costs: Y = $40,000 + $8X, where X represents machine hours. The model has R-squared = 0.88 and was estimated using data from 2,000 to 8,000 machine hours. Westbrook plans to use the model to predict costs at 15,000 machine hours. Analyze why the R-squared value does not guarantee the accuracy of this prediction and what happens to reliability when the company extrapolates beyond the relevant range.',
  choices = '["The prediction is reliable because R-squared of 0.88 means 88% of future predictions will be accurate regardless of the input value", "Extrapolation improves accuracy because the model becomes more precise as X increases beyond the sample range", "R-squared measures how well the model explains variation within the observed data range (2,000–8,000 hours), not its accuracy outside that range — at 15,000 hours, the linear relationship may break down due to overtime premiums, capacity constraints, or equipment inefficiencies that did not exist in the sample, making the $160,000 prediction unreliable despite the strong in-sample fit", "The model is invalid at any point because an R-squared below 1.00 means the regression has no predictive value"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): R-squared of 0.88 indicates that 88% of the variation in production costs is explained by machine hours within the observed range of 2,000–8,000 hours. However, R-squared is an in-sample statistic — it measures goodness of fit for the data used to build the model. At 15,000 hours (nearly double the maximum observed value), the cost structure may change nonlinearly due to overtime labor premiums, equipment wear, bottleneck effects, or subcontracting. The model predicts $160,000 ($40,000 + $8 * 15,000), but this assumes the $8/hour variable rate holds far outside the relevant range, which is analytically unsupported. Choice A misinterprets R-squared as a forward-looking accuracy guarantee. Choice B reverses the relationship — extrapolation degrades reliability. Choice D is incorrect because 0.88 represents a strong fit; less than 1.00 does not invalidate the model within its observed range.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 619;

-- ID 2970: Prospective Analysis — Fixed/variable cost trade-off and operating leverage
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgeline Manufacturing is considering automating its assembly line. Currently, fixed costs are $400,000 and variable costs are $30 per unit. After automation, fixed costs would rise to $1,200,000 while variable costs would drop to $18 per unit. The selling price is $50 per unit. Analyze how this shift from variable costs to fixed costs affects the breakeven point and the degree of operating leverage, and explain why the trade-off creates both higher risk and higher profit potential above breakeven.',
  choices = '["Automation lowers the breakeven point from 20,000 to 37,500 units and reduces operating leverage, decreasing both risk and profit potential uniformly", "Automation raises the breakeven point from 20,000 to 37,500 units but has no effect on operating leverage because leverage depends only on debt levels", "Automation raises the breakeven point from 20,000 to 37,500 units and increases the degree of operating leverage — the higher fixed cost base means more units must be sold to cover costs (greater downside risk), but each unit sold above breakeven contributes $32 instead of $20 to operating income, amplifying profits at higher volumes", "Automation eliminates the breakeven point entirely because the lower variable cost guarantees profitability at any volume"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Before automation, the contribution margin is $20/unit ($50 − $30) and breakeven is $400,000 / $20 = 20,000 units. After automation, the contribution margin rises to $32/unit ($50 − $18) and breakeven increases to $1,200,000 / $32 = 37,500 units. Despite the higher contribution margin per unit, the tripling of fixed costs drives breakeven significantly higher. The degree of operating leverage (DOL = Contribution Margin / Operating Income) also increases because the proportion of fixed costs is much larger. This means that percentage changes in sales produce amplified percentage changes in operating income — both upward and downward. Below breakeven, the company loses money faster; above it, profits grow faster per unit ($32 vs $20 per incremental unit). This risk-reward trade-off is the fundamental consequence of operating leverage. Choice A correctly notes the breakeven increase but incorrectly states leverage decreases. Choice B incorrectly confuses operating leverage with financial leverage. Choice D is incorrect because a breakeven point always exists when fixed costs are positive.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 2970;

-- ID 2972: Prospective Analysis — Operating leverage comparison under cost shock
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Industries has high operating leverage (fixed costs of $2,000,000, variable costs of $5 per unit, selling price $25 per unit). Birchwood Corp. has low operating leverage (fixed costs of $400,000, variable costs of $18 per unit, selling price $25 per unit). Both companies sell 150,000 units. Analyze which company''s operating income is more sensitive to a 10% increase in raw material costs (affecting variable costs) and explain why.',
  choices = '["Thornfield''s operating income drops more because its higher fixed costs magnify any cost increase", "Both companies experience identical percentage declines in operating income because a 10% cost increase affects all firms equally regardless of cost structure", "Neither company is affected because raw material cost increases are absorbed entirely by customers through higher prices", "Birchwood''s operating income drops more in percentage terms because its variable costs represent a larger share of total costs — a 10% increase in Birchwood''s $18 variable cost ($1.80 per unit) reduces its operating income by a larger percentage than Thornfield''s 10% increase on $5 ($0.50 per unit), since Birchwood''s contribution margin is thinner and its operating income base is smaller"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Before the cost shock, Thornfield''s operating income is 150,000 * ($25 − $5) − $2,000,000 = $1,000,000. Birchwood''s is 150,000 * ($25 − $18) − $400,000 = $650,000. A 10% variable cost increase raises Thornfield''s variable cost by $0.50 to $5.50, reducing operating income by $75,000 (7.5% decline). Birchwood''s variable cost rises by $1.80 to $19.80, reducing operating income by $270,000 (41.5% decline). Birchwood is far more sensitive because variable costs dominate its cost structure, so any variable cost shock has a proportionally larger impact. The analytical insight is that operating leverage is a double-edged sword: Thornfield is more sensitive to volume changes, but Birchwood is more sensitive to variable cost changes. Choice A incorrectly attributes sensitivity to fixed costs, which are unaffected by material cost increases. Choice B ignores cost structure differences. Choice C incorrectly assumes perfect cost pass-through.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 2972;

-- ID 2982: Prospective Analysis — DFL and interest coverage relationship
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Prescott Industries has EBIT of $800,000 and is evaluating two capital structures. Structure A has $200,000 in annual interest expense; Structure B has $600,000. Analyze the relationship between the degree of financial leverage (DFL) and the interest coverage ratio under each structure, and explain what a DFL approaching infinity signals about a company''s financial risk.',
  choices = '["Structure A has DFL of 1.33 and interest coverage of 4.0x; Structure B has DFL of 4.0 and interest coverage of 1.33x — as debt increases, DFL rises while coverage falls because both metrics measure the same underlying risk from opposite perspectives; DFL approaching infinity means EBIT barely covers interest (coverage near 1.0x), indicating that any earnings decline would push the company into inability to service its debt", "Structure A and B have identical DFL because financial leverage is determined by asset composition, not debt levels", "Higher DFL always indicates lower risk because leverage amplifies returns to equity holders", "DFL and interest coverage are unrelated metrics that measure entirely different aspects of financial performance"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): DFL = EBIT / (EBIT − Interest). Structure A: $800,000 / ($800,000 − $200,000) = 1.33. Structure B: $800,000 / ($800,000 − $600,000) = 4.0. Interest coverage = EBIT / Interest. Structure A: $800,000 / $200,000 = 4.0x. Structure B: $800,000 / $600,000 = 1.33x. These metrics are mathematically reciprocal — DFL = Coverage / (Coverage − 1). As interest expense approaches EBIT, DFL approaches infinity and coverage approaches 1.0x. A DFL near infinity means that a tiny percentage change in EBIT produces an enormous percentage change in EPS, and coverage barely exceeds 1.0x, meaning the company is one small earnings decline away from being unable to pay interest. Choice B is incorrect because DFL is directly determined by the proportion of debt financing. Choice C reverses the risk relationship — higher DFL means higher risk, not lower. Choice D is incorrect because DFL and coverage are mathematically linked.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 2982;

-- ID 2995: Prospective Analysis — Tornado diagram risk prioritization
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Haverford Corp. conducts a sensitivity analysis and produces a tornado diagram for its five-year capital project. Revenue growth has the widest bar (NPV swings from $2M to $12M), raw material costs have a moderate bar (NPV swings from $5M to $9M), and the discount rate has the narrowest bar (NPV swings from $6.5M to $7.8M). Analyze what this ranking means for management''s risk mitigation priorities and how it should guide the company''s hedging and forecasting strategy.',
  choices = '["Management should focus on the discount rate because the narrowest bar indicates the most important variable", "All three variables should receive equal attention because the tornado diagram shows they are equally important", "The tornado diagram is unreliable because sensitivity analysis cannot identify which variables matter most", "Management should allocate risk mitigation resources proportionally to bar width — revenue growth deserves the most attention because its $10M NPV range shows that project viability is most sensitive to sales volume assumptions; raw material costs warrant hedging consideration given the $4M range; the discount rate''s $1.3M range suggests financing cost variations have minimal project impact and do not justify significant hedging expenditure"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): A tornado diagram ranks input variables by their impact on the output metric (here, NPV). Bar width represents the range of NPV outcomes when each variable is independently varied across its plausible range. Revenue growth''s $10M range ($2M to $12M) dominates — it can make or break the project. This means management should prioritize revenue forecasting accuracy, invest in market research, and consider scenario planning around sales assumptions. Raw material costs ($4M range) justify hedging strategies such as forward contracts or supplier agreements. The discount rate ($1.3M range) has relatively minor impact, suggesting that moderate changes in the cost of capital are tolerable. The analytical principle is that risk mitigation resources should be directed toward the variables with the greatest impact on outcomes. Choice A inverts the interpretation — the narrowest bar indicates the least impactful variable. Choice B ignores the quantitative differences in sensitivity. Choice C incorrectly dismisses the tool''s established analytical value.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 2995;

-- ID 2997: Prospective Analysis — Sales mix shift effect on breakeven
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Lakewood Distributors sells Product Alpha (contribution margin $40, 60% of units sold) and Product Beta (contribution margin $15, 40% of units sold). Fixed costs are $600,000. Market trends are shifting demand toward Beta, and management projects the mix will change to 40% Alpha and 60% Beta. Analyze how this sales mix shift affects the weighted-average contribution margin and breakeven point, and explain why a company can become unprofitable without any decline in total units sold.',
  choices = '["The mix shift has no effect on breakeven because total units sold remain the same", "The weighted-average contribution margin increases because selling more of Beta diversifies revenue risk", "The weighted-average contribution margin drops from $30 to $25, raising breakeven from 20,000 to 24,000 units — the shift toward the lower-margin Beta dilutes the average profitability per unit, meaning the company needs 4,000 additional units just to cover the same fixed costs; if the company continues selling 20,000 units, it falls $100,000 short of breakeven despite no volume decline", "Breakeven decreases because Beta''s lower price attracts more customers, increasing total contribution"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Current weighted-average CM = (0.60 * $40) + (0.40 * $15) = $24 + $6 = $30. Breakeven = $600,000 / $30 = 20,000 units. Projected weighted-average CM = (0.40 * $40) + (0.60 * $15) = $16 + $9 = $25. Breakeven = $600,000 / $25 = 24,000 units. The 4,000-unit increase in breakeven occurs purely from the mix shift — no costs changed, no prices changed, and total volume may be unchanged. At 20,000 units, total contribution falls from $600,000 (breakeven) to $500,000 (a $100,000 operating loss). This illustrates why sales mix is a critical assumption in CVP analysis: aggregate volume metrics can mask profitability erosion when the product mix shifts toward lower-margin items. Choice A ignores the composition effect — total units are insufficient without considering per-unit profitability. Choice B reverses the math — shifting toward a lower CM product reduces the weighted average. Choice D confuses volume potential with contribution margin arithmetic.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 2997;

