-- ID 676: Stock Compensation — Grant-date fair value lock-in under ASC 718
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Waverly Technologies grants 10,000 stock options to its CFO on March 1, 2025, when the stock price is $45 and the Black-Scholes fair value is $12 per option. By December 31, 2025, the stock price has risen to $80 and the intrinsic value of each option is $35. Analyze how the timing of the grant affects the total compensation expense recognized under ASC 718, and explain why compensation cost is locked at the grant-date fair value rather than updated for subsequent stock price changes.',
  choices = '["Total compensation expense is $350,000 (10,000 * $35 intrinsic value at year-end) because ASC 718 requires mark-to-market accounting that adjusts expense for stock price movements", "Total compensation expense is $0 because stock options are not considered compensation until exercised", "Total compensation expense is $450,000 (10,000 * $45 stock price at grant date) because the full stock price is the measure of cost", "Total compensation expense is $120,000 (10,000 * $12 grant-date fair value) recognized over the vesting period — ASC 718 fixes compensation cost at the grant-date fair value because that represents the value exchanged at the commitment point; subsequent stock appreciation benefits the employee but is not an additional cost to the company, just as a cash salary does not change in value after payment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under ASC 718, compensation cost for stock options is measured at the grant-date fair value and is not subsequently remeasured for changes in the price of the underlying stock. The $12 per option fair value (determined via an option pricing model like Black-Scholes at the grant date) represents the value of the award at the point the company and employee entered into the compensation arrangement. The total $120,000 expense is recognized ratably over the requisite service period (vesting period). The stock''s appreciation to $80 increases the option''s value to the employee but does not create additional compensation expense. This approach provides reliability (avoiding income statement volatility from stock price fluctuations) and reflects the economic reality that the company''s cost was determined at the grant date. Choice A incorrectly applies mark-to-market treatment, which ASC 718 specifically rejects for equity-classified awards. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 718 requires expense recognition beginning at the grant date. Choice C confuses the stock price with the fair value of the option itself.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 676;

-- ID 677: Stock Compensation — Cliff vs graded vesting expense patterns
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Redfield Corp. grants 15,000 stock options with a grant-date fair value of $10 per option ($150,000 total) on January 1, 2025. The company is evaluating two vesting structures: cliff vesting (all 15,000 vest on December 31, 2027) vs graded vesting (5,000 vest each December 31 for 2025, 2026, and 2027). Analyze how the vesting structure affects the annual compensation expense pattern under ASC 718 and explain why graded vesting front-loads expense recognition.',
  choices = '["Both structures produce identical annual expense of $50,000 per year because total compensation cost is the same regardless of vesting structure", "Cliff vesting recognizes $50,000 per year (straight-line over 3 years); graded vesting front-loads expense — approximately $83,333 in Year 1, $41,667 in Year 2, and $25,000 in Year 3 — because ASC 718 treats each vesting tranche as a separate award with its own requisite service period, resulting in the first tranche being expensed over 1 year, the second over 2 years, and the third over 3 years", "Graded vesting defers all expense to Year 3 because no expense is recognized until each tranche vests", "Cliff vesting front-loads expense while graded vesting spreads it evenly because cliff vesting creates more uncertainty about employee retention"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 718, cliff vesting allows straight-line recognition over the single 3-year service period: $150,000 / 3 = $50,000 per year. For graded vesting, each tranche is treated as a separate award: Tranche 1 (5,000 options, $50,000) is expensed over 1 year ($50,000 in Year 1). Tranche 2 (5,000 options, $50,000) is expensed over 2 years ($25,000 per year). Tranche 3 (5,000 options, $50,000) is expensed over 3 years ($16,667 per year). Year 1 total: $50,000 + $25,000 + $16,667 = $91,667. Year 2: $25,000 + $16,667 = $41,667. Year 3: $16,667. The front-loading occurs because earlier tranches have shorter service periods, concentrating more expense in the initial years. This reflects the economic reality that the company receives service for some options sooner. Choice A is incorrect because while total cost is identical, the annual pattern differs materially. Choice C reverses the timing — expense is recognized over the service period, not deferred until vesting. Choice D reverses the front-loading relationship.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 677;

-- ID 680: Stock Compensation — ASC 805 fair value vs historical cost on post-acquisition balance sheet
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Northgate Holdings acquires Pinehurst Corp. for $25,000,000. Pinehurst''s book value of net assets is $15,000,000, but the fair values differ: land is carried at $3,000,000 (FMV $7,000,000), a customer list has no book value (FMV $2,000,000), and equipment is carried at $5,000,000 (FMV $4,500,000). Analyze why ASC 805 requires fair value measurement of identifiable assets at the acquisition date, and compare the information provided by fair value vs historical cost on the post-acquisition balance sheet.',
  choices = '["Historical cost is more relevant because it reflects actual cash paid by Pinehurst for each asset, preserving the reliability of the accounting records", "Fair value and historical cost produce the same goodwill because the total purchase price does not change", "ASC 805 requires fair value because the acquirer paid $25,000,000 based on the fair value of the business, not Pinehurst''s historical costs — recording land at $7,000,000, the customer list at $2,000,000, and equipment at $4,500,000 reflects what Northgate actually acquired and allocates the purchase price to economic values; using historical cost would understate land by $4,000,000, omit the $2,000,000 customer list entirely, and misstate the basis for future depreciation and amortization, distorting both the balance sheet and subsequent earnings", "ASC 805 allows the acquirer to choose between fair value and historical cost depending on which produces less goodwill"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): ASC 805 (Business Combinations) requires the acquirer to measure all identifiable assets acquired and liabilities assumed at their acquisition-date fair values. This principle ensures the post-acquisition balance sheet reflects the economic values the acquirer purchased, not the target''s historical transaction costs. Fair value assets: land $7M, customer list $2M, equipment $4.5M, and other net assets at book (assume $7M). Total identifiable FV net assets = $20.5M. Goodwill = $25M − $20.5M = $4.5M. Under historical cost, the customer list would be omitted, land understated by $4M, and goodwill inflated to $10M — obscuring the true drivers of the acquisition premium. Fair value measurement also establishes the proper depreciable/amortizable base for future periods. Choice A prioritizes the target''s historical perspective, which is irrelevant post-acquisition. Choice B is incorrect because goodwill is the residual and changes with the measurement basis of identifiable assets. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 805 mandates fair value — there is no choice.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 680;

-- ID 681: Stock Compensation — ASC 805 acquisition cost treatment
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Industries pays $10,000,000 for Oakmont Solutions, which has $7,000,000 in net identifiable assets at fair value. Thornfield incurs $300,000 in legal fees and $150,000 in due diligence costs related to the acquisition. Analyze why ASC 805 treats the $450,000 in transaction costs as a period expense rather than part of goodwill, and explain how this differs from the pre-ASC 805 (APB 16) approach.',
  choices = '["ASC 805 capitalizes all transaction costs into goodwill because they are necessary to complete the acquisition, producing goodwill of $3,450,000", "Under ASC 805, transaction costs are expensed as incurred, producing goodwill of $3,000,000 ($10M − $7M) — the rationale is that legal and advisory fees represent the cost of completing the transaction process, not the value of the acquired business itself; under the prior APB 16 purchase method, these costs were capitalized into goodwill, which inflated the intangible asset and obscured the true cost of the deal-making process from the acquisition''s economic value", "Transaction costs are deducted from the purchase price, reducing goodwill to $2,550,000", "ASC 805 allows the acquirer to choose between capitalizing and expensing transaction costs based on materiality"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 805, acquisition-related costs (legal fees, advisory fees, due diligence) are expensed in the period incurred, not capitalized. Goodwill = $10,000,000 − $7,000,000 = $3,000,000. The $450,000 is reported as an operating expense on the income statement. The conceptual basis is that these costs relate to the transaction process — they are incurred to find, evaluate, and close the deal — not to the fair value of the assets acquired. Under the prior APB 16 purchase method, transaction costs were included in the cost of the acquired entity and effectively became part of goodwill. The FASB changed this treatment because capitalizing deal costs conflated the price paid for the business with the cost of the acquisition process, reducing comparability and inflating reported intangible assets. Choice A applies the obsolete APB 16 approach. Choice C incorrectly reduces the purchase price rather than treating costs as a separate expense. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 805 mandates expensing — there is no election.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 681;

-- ID 3131: Stock Compensation — Measurement date significance under ASC 718
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Corp. grants stock options on June 1, 2025 (stock price $50, fair value $15/option), with a 3-year vesting period. By the vesting date (June 1, 2028), the stock price is $90, and on the exercise date (September 1, 2028), it is $95. Analyze the significance of the grant date as the measurement date under ASC 718, and compare the financial statement effects if compensation were instead measured at the vesting date or exercise date.',
  choices = '["All three measurement dates produce the same expense because stock option value does not change over time", "The exercise date is the correct measurement date because no compensation is exchanged until the employee exercises the options", "ASC 718 uses the vesting date because that is when the employee has earned the right to the options", "Measuring at the vesting date would produce higher expense ($40/option intrinsic value) and measuring at the exercise date would produce $45/option — the grant date approach produces $15/option, the lowest amount, which seems counterintuitive but reflects GAAP''s preference for reliability over relevance; the grant date is the mutual commitment point where both parties agree to terms, and subsequent price changes represent market movements, not additional compensation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): ASC 718 establishes the grant date as the measurement date for equity-classified awards because it is the date of mutual understanding of the key terms. At grant date, fair value is $15/option (measured by an option pricing model). If measurement occurred at the vesting date, intrinsic value would be $40 ($90 − $50), and at the exercise date, $45 ($95 − $50). The grant-date approach produces the most reliable and least volatile measure because it avoids remeasuring compensation expense for stock market fluctuations over the service period. The FASB chose the grant date over alternatives because (1) it is the point of mutual commitment, (2) it provides consistency across reporting periods, and (3) subsequent stock price changes reflect capital market returns to the employee, not incremental compensation from the employer. Choice A is incorrect because option value changes significantly over time. Choice B describes the now-obsolete APB 25 intrinsic value method logic. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 718 explicitly selects the grant date, not the vesting date.',
  correct_index = 3
WHERE id = 3131;

-- ID 3141: Stock Compensation — Performance vs market condition treatment
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Evergreen Technologies grants 20,000 stock options with two independent vesting conditions: (1) a performance condition requiring cumulative revenue to exceed $50,000,000 over three years, and (2) a market condition requiring the stock price to reach $100 by December 31, 2027 (current price $65). At the end of 2027, revenue is only $42,000,000 (performance condition not met) and the stock price is $85 (market condition not met). Analyze how ASC 718 treats the failure to meet each condition differently and explain the conceptual rationale for this asymmetry.',
  choices = '["Both conditions are treated identically — all previously recognized expense is reversed because neither condition was met", "The performance condition failure requires reversal of all previously recognized compensation expense, but the market condition failure does not — expense recognized for the market condition remains in the income statement because ASC 718 incorporates the probability of meeting market conditions into the grant-date fair value using option pricing models, so the expected cost was already risk-adjusted at inception; performance conditions cannot be priced by the market, so their probability is estimated each period and adjusted as outcomes become clearer", "Neither condition affects expense recognition because stock option expense is always based solely on the service period", "The market condition failure triggers expense reversal while the performance condition failure does not, because market conditions are more objectively measurable"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 718, performance conditions (revenue targets, earnings goals) and market conditions (stock price thresholds) receive fundamentally different accounting treatment. For performance conditions, the company estimates the probability of achievement each period and adjusts cumulative expense accordingly — if the condition is ultimately not met, all previously recognized expense is reversed. For market conditions, the probability of achievement is incorporated into the grant-date fair value through option pricing models (e.g., Monte Carlo simulation). The expense is recognized over the service period regardless of whether the market condition is achieved. The rationale is that option pricing models can reliably incorporate market-based probabilities, so the "expected cost" already reflects the risk of non-achievement. Performance conditions are entity-specific and cannot be priced by the market, requiring period-by-period probability assessment. Choice A incorrectly applies identical treatment. Choice C ignores the impact of conditions on expense. Choice D reverses the treatment — performance conditions trigger reversal, not market conditions.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 3141;

-- ID 3156: Stock Compensation — Option repricing incremental cost analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Crestview Biotech reprices 50,000 underwater stock options from an exercise price of $50 to $30 when the stock price is $28. The original options had a fair value of $8 each at the initial grant date; at the repricing date, the original options have a fair value of $3 each and the modified options have a fair value of $9 each. Analyze the incremental compensation cost from this modification under ASC 718 and explain how the repricing affects total expense over the remaining 2-year vesting period.',
  choices = '["Incremental cost is $450,000 (50,000 * $9 new fair value) because the original grant is completely replaced", "Incremental cost is $50,000 (50,000 * ($3 − $8) savings) because the lower stock price reduced the option value", "Incremental cost is $300,000 (50,000 * ($9 − $3)) — ASC 718 measures modification cost as the excess of the new fair value over the old fair value at the modification date; the company continues recognizing the original $8 fair value over the original vesting period plus the $6 incremental value over the remaining 2-year vesting period, increasing total expense from $400,000 to $700,000", "No incremental cost because underwater options have zero value and repricing merely restores their original terms"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 718, a repricing is a modification that requires measuring incremental compensation cost as the excess of the modified award''s fair value over the original award''s fair value, both measured at the modification date. Modified FV: $9/option. Original FV at modification date: $3/option. Incremental: $9 − $3 = $6/option * 50,000 = $300,000. The original grant-date fair value ($8/option, $400,000 total) continues to be recognized over the original vesting schedule. The $300,000 incremental cost is recognized over the remaining 2-year vesting period ($150,000/year). Total expense increases from $400,000 to $700,000. The repricing benefits employees by lowering the exercise price, but ASC 718 ensures the full economic cost of this benefit transfer is recognized. Choice A incorrectly treats the repricing as a completely new grant, ignoring the original award''s continuing expense. Choice B misapplies the calculation direction — the fair value increased, not decreased. Choice D is incorrect because underwater options retain time value and modification cost must be recognized.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 3156;

